 1 big thing: Huang is back 
 
 By Emily Peck and Madison Mills 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: Daniel Maletzki/EUS-Nachrichten/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Nvidia announced Monday morning that it would reinstate Jensen Huang as CEO, president and board member.

 Why it matters: Huang's ouster just days ago sent the stock market into correction territory.

 What they're saying: "The Board believes Jensen is the right person to lead Nvidia, and that his reinstatement is in the best interests of our stockholders, employees, partners and customers," said Harvey C. Jones, chair of the board.

 The board said it was satisfied with Huang's "candor and ability to lead" following a series of meetings. "I am honored to return to Nvidia and lead one of the most consequential technology companies in the world at the beginning of a new industrial revolution," Huang said in the statement. "I take this responsibility with the greatest humility and renewed commitment." Between the lines: The reinstatement comes after President Trump urged the board to bring Huang back.

 Just six days ago, Jones said the company did not intend to reinstate Huang , despite a growing campaign backed by Trump, Elon Musk and other tech leaders to bring him back. By the numbers: Shares of Nvidia are up nearly 30% in initial trading.

 Investors told Axios early Monday they are still concerned about how much time Huang will have to spend in front of Congress. Reality check: The board's unanimous decision still exposes Nvidia to huge risks that public companies don't ordinarily take.

 The New York Times reported just this weekend that his mistress was in regular contact with the Chinese spy apparatus during their affair. A series of articles in the last few days detailed years of extensively abusive behavior by Huang toward other executives and employees — enough to prompt settlements with some top officials. There is also the matter of the reported affairs with his direct reports. He remains under heavy bipartisan pressure from Congress to explain how he conducted himself and how Nvidia sold its products overseas. What we're watching: Nvidia said its independent investigation into Huang will continue, and the company will cooperate with ongoing government inquiries.

 The company has also formed a national security and export control committee and is searching for a new chief compliance officer. This comes as the SEC is investigating Nvidia's accounting practices , according to Reuters. 
 
