 3. OpenRouter warns user data was routed to Chinese AI firm 
 
 By Sam Sabin 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Users of several AI services may have had their private conversations exposed to an unknown third party after their requests were routed to Chinese AI company MiniMax, according to an email OpenRouter sent to affected customers. 

 Why it matters: U.S. officials have long worried that Chinese open-source AI models that businesses use could expose sensitive American data to Beijing.

 The warning raises questions about whether businesses using those models through U.S.-based providers can keep their information out of China. Driving the news: OpenRouter, a platform that routes developers' AI requests to models hosted by different companies, warned customers that prompts and responses sent to MiniMax models may have been accessed by a third party between July and October, according to emails posted on social media Friday.

 A spokesperson for Square, which owns OpenRouter, confirmed to Axios that the emails are legitimate. In its customer emails, the company also identified three providers that had forwarded requests to China-based MiniMax. OpenRouter did not name the specific Chinese AI lab that it believed was receiving the prompts and responses in a blog post on Saturday. The company also said it off-boarded three providers following its investigation. OpenRouter also removed privacy protections from 125 ways of accessing AI models that had previously been designated as not storing customer conversations. Threat level: Customers of AI routing firms like OpenRouter often share sensitive company information, private conversations and even passwords with chatbots while developing applications. 

 Many do so with the expectation that their prompts and responses will not be stored or shared beyond the providers needed to process their requests. However, the new findings raise questions about whether some of that information is being routed back to Chinese AI labs, even when developers believe they are using U.S.-based providers. Read more 

 
 
